  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "Fahrwerk",
    "overview": "Fahrwerk is the running gear of a storage yard: the part that carries the daily load and is only noticed when it fails. Parkrr's interface follows that idea. A slate ground with a petrol tint carries dense operational screens (persons, vehicles, extra costs, invoices, the tax year) without decoration competing for attention. Petrol marks what can be acted on, copper marks money, amber marks a state that needs a look, and the faint garage-plan linework behind every page ties the tool to its spatial core, the scale hall planner.\n\nThe app is mobile-first and operated with one hand on a phone in the yard as often as at a desk. Controls are direct (one-tap sliders, segmented switches, whole-card navigation), numbers line up, and every state is readable without color. Dark is the default and is designed on its own terms; light is a full counterpart, not an inversion.\n\nThe standalone review workbenches (security audit, product roadmap, before/after comparisons) share the same world: the same slate and petrol, the same three typefaces, the same plan linework. They differ in density and composition, not in material.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Slate ground, petrol action, copper money, amber state.",
      "Three voices: Bricolage Grotesque for numbers and headings, Hanken Grotesk for reading, JetBrains Mono for labels and data.",
      "One-tap controls and whole-card targets over forms and small buttons.",
      "Garage-plan linework as the recurring signature, always behind content.",
      "Every state carried by text, shape or position as well as color."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Copper-Is-Money Rule",
        "body": "Kupfer appears only where money is meant: a balance, an amount owed, the current month of a money chart. Never for decoration, never for a warning.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Amber-Is-State Rule",
        "body": "Amber describes a state (reserved, due soon, backup stale). It is not a second accent.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Paper-Stays-Paper Rule",
        "body": "The invoice preview uses the Beleg ink in every theme, because the operator must read on screen exactly what the customer receives.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Restrained Petrol Rule",
        "body": "Petrol directs attention; it does not wash large areas. Large surfaces stay slate or paper.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Ladder Rule",
        "body": "Every size comes from the nine app steps above. Three exceptions are deliberate: the planner's zoom-dependent block labels (0.5–0.58rem), the icon-button glyph and invoice title (1.2rem), and single display figures between 1.3 and 2.5rem that each carry their own level of the hierarchy.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Rule",
        "body": "Money, counts and dates that line up use tabular numerals.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Border-Before-Shadow Rule",
        "body": "Establish structure with rules and surface contrast first. Workbenches stay flat; app cards get only the soft card shadow; only true overlays float.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** keep copper for money and amber for state, and pair every color with text, an icon or a position.",
      "**Do** use the nine-step type ladder and tabular numerals for figures that line up.",
      "**Do** make the common action one tap: sliders, whole-card targets, tap-to-place in the planner.",
      "**Do** keep icons in the shared SVG set and draw new ones with the same 1.8px stroke.",
      "**Do** let the garage-plan linework sit behind content, with a scrim under page heads.",
      "**Do** design dark and light separately and measure every ink/ground pair (WCAG AA)."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** use copper or amber as decoration, and don't use petrol as a large fill.",
      "**Don't** introduce emoji or font-dependent Unicode glyphs as icons.",
      "**Don't** add font sizes outside the ladder, or radii outside the scale, without documenting why.",
      "**Don't** put the invoice preview into theme colors.",
      "**Don't** add external fonts, CDNs or hosted services; all assets are local.",
      "**Don't** introduce raster imagery into the workbenches without a documented need and provenance."
    ]
  }
}
